<!-- ===== SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS ===== -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-title-wrap">
      <div class="section-number">6</div>
      <div>
        <h2>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
        <div class="section-desc">Synthesized actionable insights for energy policymakers on subsidy design, tax calibration, and price stabilization</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding-item">
    <div class="finding-num">1</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div><span class="tag tag-policy">Pass-Through</span><span class="tag tag-risk">Fiscal Risk</span></div>
      <h4>Subsidies buffer absolute prices but not proportional shocks</h4>
      <p>All four subsidy tiers recorded nearly identical percentage price increases (~69%) during the 2020→2022 Brent Crude surge, despite absolute price gaps of over $3.00/L. This exposes the fundamental limitation of flat-rate subsidy designs: they maintain lower price floors but do not reduce the <em>proportional</em> pass-through from crude markets. The 2022 energy crisis imposed disproportionate fiscal costs on governments with large subsidy programs, as maintaining fixed consumer prices required absorbing the full commodity shock.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding-item">
    <div class="finding-num">2</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div><span class="tag tag-policy">Subsidy Design</span></div>
      <h4>Very-high-subsidy regimes achieve near-zero consumer prices at extreme fiscal cost</h4>
      <p>Countries in the "Very High" subsidy tier (Venezuela, Iran, Libya, Algeria) maintain average prices of just $0.15/L — 95.7% below low-subsidy markets. While politically durable, this approach costs governments ~$3.30/L per liter sold and is fiscally sustainable only for major crude exporters. The 10 countries in this tier represent a distinct policy model (producer-state price repression) that should not be generalized to net-importing economies.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding-item">
    <div class="finding-num">3</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div><span class="tag tag-equity">Equity</span><span class="tag tag-policy">Tax Design</span></div>
      <h4>High-income countries systematically use fuel taxation as a revenue and externality tool</h4>
      <p>High-income countries carry a 34.7% average tax-to-price ratio versus 21–23% for middle and low-income countries. European countries (average tax 34.6%) and Oceania (38.7%) use elevated fuel taxes to internalize carbon externalities, fund transport infrastructure, and generate general revenue. The regional spread in tax rates — from 18.2% (South America) to 38.7% (Oceania) — shows that fiscal philosophy, not commodity costs, drives the majority of cross-regional price differentials.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding-item">
    <div class="finding-num">4</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div><span class="tag tag-risk">Volatility</span></div>
      <h4>Regional price spreads are widening: from $1.61/L in 2020 to $2.87/L in 2026</h4>
      <p>The gap between the highest-priced (Europe: $4.29/L) and lowest-priced (Middle East: $1.42/L) regions has nearly doubled in dollar terms over the period, even as percentage changes have been uniform. This divergence reflects the amplifying effect of taxes on top of rising crude: a 78% price increase applied to a $2.40 base (Europe 2020) adds $1.88/L, while the same percentage on a $0.80 base (Middle East 2020) adds only $0.63/L.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding-item">
    <div class="finding-num">5</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div><span class="tag tag-equity">Equity</span><span class="tag tag-risk">Food Security</span></div>
      <h4>LPG remains the most affordable fuel type globally — critical for low-income household energy access</h4>
      <p>LPG trades at an average 25% discount to petrol ($1.71/L vs. $2.28/L) and is the primary cooking and heating fuel for hundreds of millions of low-income households. Middle East LPG ($0.92/L) and South America LPG ($1.07/L) are particularly accessible. Policymakers targeting energy poverty should consider LPG-specific subsidy structures as more cost-effective than broad petrol subsidies, given LPG's household welfare significance and smaller environmental footprint than kerosene alternatives.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding-item">
    <div class="finding-num">6</div>
    <div class="finding-body">
      <div><span class="tag tag-policy">Stabilization</span><span class="tag tag-risk">Design Recommendation</span></div>
      <h4>Price stabilization funds and automatic adjustment mechanisms outperform fixed subsidies</h4>
      <p>The uniform pass-through finding (Finding 1) suggests that well-designed price stabilization funds — which collect contributions when crude is below a threshold and disburse when crude spikes above it — can smooth volatility without requiring permanent fiscal commitments. Countries maintaining fixed nominal subsidies exposed themselves to the full fiscal shock of the 2022 crisis. A rules-based automatic adjustment formula (e.g., passing through a fixed percentage of crude moves above/below a band) would achieve consumer price stability while maintaining long-run fiscal sustainability.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

